    "proof_summary": "Partition the two keys by the explorer who first collects each. For each of the four allocations, exhaustively enumerate each explorer's key visit order and sum shortest-path segment lengths from its start through those keys to the exit. The minimum over allocations of the larger of the two route lengths is30. The corresponding routes have lengths30 and29, so they are jointly executable: one shared die gives each explorer up to the same roll each round; both follow their routes and may wait. Therefore under full map knowledge and optimal movement, success withinN rounds is exactly P(sum ofN independent fair d6 rolls >=30). Victory is checked during the final round before the dragon advances.",
    "probability_assumptions": [
      "Mathematically fair independent d6 rolls.",
      "Players know the whole map and follow the optimal routes without movement errors.",
      "Movement can be stopped and pawns do not block each other.",
      "Key collection is immediate and global.",
      "These are optimal-play model results, not empirical child-playtest or fairness claims."
    ],
